  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Isiolo Humanitarian Assistance Project</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project will expand on the current humanitarian response work undertaken by the Diocese&#x2019;s Development Office and build on the ongoing response work that has been going on since December 2008 . This component is an attempt to contribute to the alleviation of the suffering as a result of the escalation of the drought situation in Northern Kenya and particularly in Isiolo District. The additional activities is in response to the continuing deterioration of the drought situation and food security situation in Northern Kenya and also an attempt to respond to the appeal by the Government of Kenya (GoK) following the declaration of the drought and food crisis a national disaster on 16th January 2009. This project component will reach an additional 400 direct beneficiaries benefiting from the &#x201C;take home ration&#x201D; from the previous number of 800 children  and thus totalling 1200children benefiting from the &#x201C;take home ration&#x201D;. Additionally, the Diocese of Isiolo will target an additional 10,000 more people in its health facilities for treatment of common drought diseases. Already, an appeal has been made to contain an outbreak of black fever in Merti and Sericho Divisions. CAFOD is increasing it s support in health care provision to correspond to the improved staff capacity of the Diocese to arrest this additional threats to the lives of the communities in Isiolo who will now benefit either directly or indirectly from improved access to essential medical assistance from clinics run by the Diocese with mobile outreach clinics, 

By working more closely with the Arid Land resource Ministry, The Diocese  will help to increase and improved access to safe water through water trucking through the provision of diesel fuel to support the Government of Kenya (GoK) carry out water tankering operations in locations hardest hit by drought.  These activities will be undertaken concurrently with the ongoing drought and food crisis response programme</narrative>
  </description>
